[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a complete, **v6-compatible** example that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file container for vector tiles) with the official `pmtiles` library.  
2. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol so MapLibre can fetch the tiles directly from the file.  
3. Add the PMTiles file as a **vector source** to the map.  
4. Create a **line layer** that draws a line (or road, trail, etc.) from one of the source’s layers.

You can copy-paste the whole thing into an `index.html` file and open it in a browser (or serve it locally with any static-file server).

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles line layer</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre CSS (v6) -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
  <div id="map"></div>

  <!-- The script is an ES module (v6 only) -->
  <script type="module">
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣  Imports – MapLibre GL JS (v6) and the PMTiles helper library
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
    import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@2.7.0/dist/index.mjs';

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Register the pmtiles:// protocol
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Replace this URL with the location of *your* .pmtiles file.
    const pmtilesUrl = 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MapLibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/pmtiles/example.pmtiles';

    // Create a PMTiles object and tell it to handle the custom protocol.
    const pm = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
    pm.addProtocol();   // now fetch('pmtiles://example.pmtiles') works

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Initialise the map
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {
        version: 8,
        sources: {},          // we’ll add our PMTiles source later
        layers: []            // and our line layer later
      },
      center: [0, 0],         // lon, lat – adjust to your data’s extent
      zoom: 2,
      hash: true
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  When the map is ready, add the PMTiles vector source
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    map.on('load', () => {
      // The source name can be anything you like.
      const sourceId = 'my-pmtiles-source';

      map.addSource(sourceId, {
        type: 'vector',
        // The URL uses the custom protocol we registered above.
        // The part after the double slash must match the filename you gave
        // to `new PMTiles(...)`.  In the example we used the full URL, so we
        // can just reuse the same string.
        url: `pmtiles://${pmtilesUrl}`
      });

      // --------------------------------------------------------------
      // 6️⃣  Add a line layer that draws from a source layer inside the PMTiles
      // --------------------------------------------------------------
      // You need to know the name of the layer that contains the line
      // geometry inside the vector tiles.  You can discover it with
      // `maplibre-gl`’s `inspect` tool, or by opening the .pmtiles file
      // with `pmtiles inspect` on the command line.
      const sourceLayerName = 'roads';   // <-- change to your actual layer name

      map.addLayer({
        id: 'my-line-layer',
        type: 'line',
        source: sourceId,
        'source-layer': sourceLayerName,

        // Simple styling – feel free to tweak any paint properties.
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#ff6600',
          'line-width': [
            'interpolate',
            ['linear'],
            ['zoom'],
            5, 0.5,
            12, 2,
            16, 6
          ],
          'line-opacity': 0.9
        },

        // Optional layout properties
        layout: {
          'line-cap': 'round',
          'line-join': 'round'
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## How the pieces fit together

| Step | What it does | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **Import MapLibre (v6)** | `import * as maplibregl …` | v6 ships only as an ES-module; there is no default export. |
| **Import PMTiles** | `import { PMTiles } …` | The `pmtiles` library implements a custom fetch protocol (`pmtiles://`). |
| **`pm.addProtocol()`** | Registers a global fetch handler for `pmtiles://` URLs. | After this call, any MapLibre source that points to `pmtiles://…` will automatically read tiles from the file. |
| **`map.addSource({type:'vector', url:'pmtiles://…' })`** | Declares a vector source that reads its tiles from the PMTiles container. | The source behaves exactly like a remote TileJSON/XYZ source, but all tiles are served from the single file. |
| **`map.addLayer({type:'line', source:'…', 'source-layer':… })`** | Draws a line layer using the vector data. | The `source-layer` name must match a layer that exists inside the PMTiles file (e.g., `roads`, `paths`, `rail`). |
| **Paint & layout** | Sets colour, width, opacity, caps, joins, etc. | Gives you a visible line that scales nicely with zoom. |

---

## Tips & Gotchas

| Issue | Fix / Recommendation |
|-------|-----------------------|
| **Don’t know the source-layer name** | Open the PMTiles file with the CLI: `npx pmtiles inspect path/to/file.pmtiles`. It prints a list of layer names. |
| **CORS errors when the PMTiles file is on another domain** | The PMTiles file is fetched via `fetch()`. The server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your origin). If you host it yourself, add that header. |
| **Large PMTiles files cause a long initial download** | The library streams only the needed byte ranges, so the whole file isn’t downloaded up-front. Still, make sure the server supports HTTP range requests (`Accept-Ranges: bytes`). |
| **Using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.)** | The CDN example above works without extra configuration. In a bundler you still need the same imports, but you may also have to call `setWorkerUrl()` once (see the v6 migration guide) if you’re using a custom build of MapLibre. |
| **Styling based on feature properties** | Use an expression in the `paint` object, e.g. `'line-color': ['match', ['get', 'class'], 'highway', '#ff0000', '#00ff00']`. |
| **Multiple line layers from the same source** | Just add more `map.addLayer()` calls with different `id`s and different filter expressions. |

---

## TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

If you already have a MapLibre map instance (`map`) and you just want the two-line commands that hook up a PMTiles source and a line layer:

```js
import { PMTiles } from 'pmtiles';

// 1️⃣ Register the protocol (do this once)
new PMTiles('https://example.com/data.pmtiles').addProtocol();

// 2️⃣ Add the vector source
map.addSource('pmtiles-src', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/data.pmtiles'
});

// 3️⃣ Add the line layer (replace 'roads' with your actual source-layer)
map.addLayer({
  id: 'pmtiles-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'pmtiles-src',
  'source-layer': 'roads',
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#ff6600',
    'line-width': 2
  }
});
```

That’s all you need to get a line layer rendered from a PMTiles vector-tile file in MapLibre GL JS v6. Happy mapping!

Below is a complete, **v6-compatible** example that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file container for vector tiles) in the browser.  
2. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol that MapLibre GL JS understands.  
3. Add the PMTiles file as a **vector source**.  
4. Create a **line layer** that draws features from that source.

You can copy-paste the HTML into a file (e.g. `index.html`) and open it in a browser.  
If you are using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.) the same code works – just import the
modules instead of loading them from a CDN and call `setWorkerUrl()` once (see the
migration guide if you need that).

---

## 1️⃣ HTML + ESM script (CDN)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS + PMTiles demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <!-- MapLibre GL CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>

<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Import the libraries (ESM-only builds)
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js';

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Register the `pmtiles://` protocol
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // The PMTiles library implements a fetch-like handler that MapLibre can call.
  // It must be registered *before* you add any source that uses the protocol.
  const pmtilesUrl = 'https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-file.pmtiles'; // ← change this
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);

  // `maplibregl.addProtocol` is a static helper that lets MapLibre resolve
  // custom URL schemes. The handler receives a request object and must return
  // a Promise that resolves to `{ data: Uint8Array, cacheControl?: string, expires?: string }`.
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', async (request) => {
    // `request` contains `{ url, range, ... }`. The PMTiles instance knows how
    // to serve the requested byte range.
    const resp = await pmtiles.get(request);
    if (resp.error) throw new Error(resp.error);
    return { data: resp.data };
  });

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Create the map
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add our PMTiles source later
      layers: []            // we’ll add our line layer later
    },
    center: [0, 0],         // lon, lat
    zoom: 2,
    hash: true
  });

  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ When the map is ready, add the PMTiles vector source
  // ------------------------------------------------------------
  map.once('load', () => {
    // The source points to the PMTiles file via the custom protocol.
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      // The URL must start with `pmtiles://` so the handler above is used.
      url: `pmtiles://${pmtilesUrl}`
    });

    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ Add a line layer that draws from the source
    // ------------------------------------------------------------
    // You need to know the *source-layer* name that lives inside the vector tiles.
    // You can discover it with `pmtiles inspect` or by opening the file in a
    // viewer (e.g. https://viewer.protomaps.com/).  For this example we assume the
    // layer is called "roads".
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      // The name of the layer inside the vector tile set.
      'source-layer': 'roads',
      layout: {
        // Optional: smooth joins between line segments
        'line-join': 'round',
        'line-cap': 'round'
      },
      paint: {
        // Simple styling – a thick blue line
        'line-color': '#1E90FF',
        'line-width': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['zoom'],
          5, 1,
          12, 4,
          18, 12
        ]
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | Code | Why it matters |
|------|------|----------------|
| **Import** | `import * as maplibregl …` and `import { PMTiles } …` | v6 ships only as an ES module (`type="module"`). |
| **Protocol registration** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', …)` | MapLibre only knows `http://`, `https://`, `mapbox://`, etc. By registering `pmtiles://` we tell it how to fetch the byte ranges from the PMTiles file. |
| **Map creation** | `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` | Standard map init. The `style` object can be empty; we’ll add sources/layers later. |
| **Add source** | `map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', { type: 'vector', url: 'pmtiles://…' })` | Declares a vector source that reads its tiles from the PMTiles container. |
| **Add line layer** | `map.addLayer({ id: 'my-lines', type: 'line', source: 'my-pmtiles-source', 'source-layer': 'roads', … })` | Renders the features from the `roads` layer inside the PMTiles tiles as a styled line. |
| **Paint & layout** | `line-color`, `line-width`, `line-join`, `line-cap` | Simple visual styling; you can replace these with any MapLibre GL style expressions you like. |

