## 2️⃣ Using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.)

If you prefer a build-tool workflow, the same logic applies; you just import the modules from `node_modules` instead of the CDN URLs.

```bash
# Install the packages
npm i maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
```

```js
// src/main.js   (or .ts)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles } from '@protomaps/pmtiles';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// ---- Bundler-specific worker URL (required for Vite, webpack, etc.) ----
import { setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------

const pmtilesUrl = '/data/my-tiles.pmtiles'; // relative to your dev server
const pmtiles = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', async (req) => {
  const resp = await pmtiles.get(req);
  if (resp.error) throw new Error(resp.error);
  return { data: resp.data };
});

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: { version: 8, sources: {}, layers: [] },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.once('load', () => {
  map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: `pmtiles://${pmtilesUrl}`
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
    'source-layer': 'roads',
    layout: { 'line-join': 'round', 'line-cap': 'round' },
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#1E90FF',
      'line-width': [
        'interpolate',
        ['linear'],
        ['zoom'],
        5, 1,
        12, 4,
        18, 12
      ]
    }
  });
});
```

**Key bundler notes**

* **`setWorkerUrl()`** – required for any non-CDN build so the map can load its WebWorker.  
* **`?worker&url`** – Vite’s special import query that returns the worker’s URL.  
* For webpack 5+ you can use the same `?worker&url` syntax (or `new URL(..., import.meta.url)`).

---

## 3️⃣ Finding the correct `source-layer` name

The name you put in `'source-layer'` must match a layer that exists inside the vector tiles. You can discover it in several ways:

* **Command-line**: `pmtiles inspect path/to/file.pmtiles` (the output lists layer names).  
* **Online viewer**: Upload the file to https://viewer.protomaps.com/ – the UI shows the layer list.  
* **Programmatic**: Load the PMTiles file with the library and call `pmtiles.getMetadata()`; the returned JSON contains a `vector_layers` array with `id` fields.

If you use the wrong name, the layer will simply render nothing (no error is thrown).

---

## 4️⃣ Full-screen minimal example (CDN)

If you want the absolute smallest thing that works, copy this into a file and replace `YOUR_DOMAIN/.../mytiles.pmtiles` with the URL of your PMTiles file:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>PMTiles line demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet">
  <style>#map{width:100vw;height:100vh;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js';

  const pmtilesUrl = 'https://YOUR_DOMAIN/path/to/your-file.pmtiles';
  const pm = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', async (req) => {
    const r = await pm.get(req);
    if (r.error) throw new Error(r.error);
    return { data: r.data };
  });

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: { version: 8, sources: {}, layers: [] },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  map.once('load', () => {
    map.addSource('pmtiles-src', { type: 'vector', url: `pmtiles://${pmtilesUrl}` });
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'line',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'pmtiles-src',
      'source-layer': 'roads',   // <-- change to your actual layer name
      paint: { 'line-color': '#ff6600', 'line-width': 3 }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, and you should see the line features from the PMTiles source rendered on the map.

---

### 🎉 That's it!

* Register the `pmtiles://` protocol with `maplibregl.addProtocol`.  
* Add the PMTiles file as a **vector source** (`type: 'vector'`).  
* Create a **line layer** that points at that source and the correct `source-layer`.  
* (If you’re using a bundler, remember the one-time `setWorkerUrl()` call.)

Feel free to tweak the `paint
